<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austrian Empire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austrian Empire was the state ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> from 1804, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> proclaimed himself Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, until 1867. It included Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After its defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866 it was reorganised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austrian-empire.jpg" alt="Austrian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThrashedParanoid and Peregrine981. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Habsburg_Monarchy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…or Compromise, of 1867 was the product of negotiations between Emperor Franz Joseph I and Hungarian liberal leaders, above all Ferenc Deák and Count Gyula Andrássy, who recognised that a weakened <b>Austrian Empire</b> needed Hungarian cooperation and that Hungary could extract far-reaching autonomy from a dynasty in crisis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…incorporated into the Final Act of the Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the <b>Austrian Empire</b> and the Kingdom of Prussia for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, Saxony, Württemberg and Hanover, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free…</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its central problem, how to govern many peoples with distinct laws and languages under one dynasty, passed to the <b>Austrian Empire</b> and Austria-Hungary, and still informs debates about federalism and nationality in Central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1804 Moravia was a crown land of the <b>Austrian Empire</b> and, after 1867, of the Austrian half of Austria-Hungary, with its own diet and governor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Textile mills opened from the 1860s, and a customs treaty with the <b>Austrian Empire</b> from 1852 tied the economy to the Habsburg market until 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1817 he came to Oberndorf, a village on the Salzach river, then in the <b>Austrian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a></li></ul></section>
